[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Linear Search Algorithm"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linear search, also known as sequential search, is a simple algorithm for finding a target value within a list. It sequentially checks each element of the list until a match is found or the entire list has been searched."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Linear Search Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The linear search algorithm operates as follows:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Start at the first element of the list."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Compare the target value with the current element."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If the target value matches the current element, return the index of the element."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If the target value does not match the current element, move to the next element in the list."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Repeat steps 2-4 until either the target value is found or the end of the list is reached."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If the target value is not found after searching the entire list, return a value indicating that the target is not present (e.g., -1)."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Pseudocode"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"function linearSearch(list, target):
  for each element in list:
    if element equals target:
      return index of element
  return -1"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Implementation (Python)"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"def linear_search(list, target):
  for i in range(len(list)):
    if list[i] == target:
      return i
  return -1

# Example usage:
my_list = [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
target_value = 9
index = linear_search(my_list, target_value)
if index != -1:
  print(f"Target {target_value} found at index {index}")
else:
  print(f"Target {target_value} not found in the list")"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Time Complexity"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The time complexity of linear search is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the list. In the worst-case scenario (when the target value is not in the list or is the last element), the algorithm must examine every element in the list."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Space Complexity"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The space complexity of linear search is O(1), as it only requires a constant amount of extra space, regardless of the size of the input list."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages and Disadvantages"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Advantages: Simple to implement, works on unsorted lists."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Disadvantages: Inefficient for large lists compared to more advanced search algorithms like binary search (which requires a sorted list)."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Linear Search"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linear search is suitable for:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Small lists where the overhead of more complex algorithms outweighs the benefits."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unsorted lists where other search algorithms cannot be applied directly."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Situations where simplicity and ease of implementation are prioritized over performance."}]
